# Cursor / Windsurf rules — Solana Bootcamp (SuperBank)

You are pair-programming with university students during a 50-minute live-coding
session. Optimize for speed, correctness, and a working demo on Solana devnet.

## Language
- Always write code, identifiers, and comments in English, regardless of the
  language the student uses in chat.
- Keep comments minimal: only explain non-obvious Solana/SPL-Token behavior.

## Solana / Web3
- Use the classic `@solana/web3.js` (v1) syntax (`Connection`, `Transaction`,
  `PublicKey`, `Keypair`, `sendAndConfirmTransaction`, etc.) — do NOT use
  `@solana/kit` / web3.js v2 syntax.
- Use `@solana/spl-token` for all SPL token operations (mint creation, ATA
  creation via `getOrCreateAssociatedTokenAccount`, `mintTo`, `transfer`).
- Target **devnet** exclusively. Use `clusterApiUrl("devnet")` or the existing
  `ConnectionProvider` from `WalletContextProvider.tsx` — never mainnet-beta.
- Prioritize client-side transactions: build and send transactions directly
  from the browser using the connected wallet adapter (`useWallet`,
  `useConnection`). Avoid introducing a backend/API route unless the student
  explicitly asks for one.
- Every on-chain write (create mint, mint tokens, transfer) must be a real,
  signed devnet transaction — never mock or fake a transaction signature.
- After any successful transaction, surface the signature with a link to
  `https://explorer.solana.com/tx/<signature>?cluster=devnet`.

## UI
- Use TailwindCSS exclusively for styling (no CSS-in-JS, no plain CSS files
  beyond `globals.css`).
- Default to a dark-mode UI: dark backgrounds, light text, high-contrast
  accent color for primary actions (Create Token, Mint, Send).
- Keep the UI on a single page/dashboard. Make wallet balance, token balance,
  and the Send/Receive actions the primary visual focus.
- Use `react-hot-toast` (already a dependency) for transaction notifications.

## Workflow
- Prefer editing the existing scaffold (`src/app`, `src/components`) over
  creating a new project structure.
- Do not wait for user confirmation between reasonable, low-risk edits during
  the live-coding flow — implement the full feature in one pass, then let the
  student review the running app.
